## Unit of Work vs Transaction

## Transaction

A database transaction is a low-level mechanism:
```
- BEGIN TRANSACTION
- Execute SQL
- COMMIT or ROLLBACK
```
It's a DB concept. Nothing in your domain layer knows about it.

## Unit of Work

A UoW is a higher-level pattern that:
```
1. Tracks all objects loaded from the DB (identity map)
2. Records every change made to those objects
3. At commit: generates SQL for all changes and executes them in one transaction
```

```typescript
interface UnitOfWork {
  users: UserRepository;
  orders: OrderRepository;
  commit(): Promise<void>;
  rollback(): Promise<void>;
}

// Usage
async function placeOrder(userId: string, items: Item[]) {
  const uow = new UnitOfWork(db);

  const user = await uow.users.findById(userId);
  user.incrementOrderCount();

  const order = Order.create(userId, items);
  await uow.orders.save(order);

  // Both user update AND order insert happen in one transaction
  await uow.commit();
}
```

## Key Differences

```
                  Transaction         Unit of Work
─────────────────────────────────────────────────
Level             DB / infrastructure  Application layer
Knowledge         SQL statements       Domain objects
Change tracking   None                 Tracks dirty objects
Scope             Explicit SQL block   Business operation
Flush             Immediate            Deferred (batched)
```

## Benefits

```
✅ Atomic business operations spanning multiple aggregates
✅ Deferred writes reduce DB round-trips
✅ Identity map prevents loading the same entity twice
✅ Explicit commit boundary makes the operation intent clear
```

