## Program to Interfaces, Not Implementations

## The Problem

```typescript
// ❌ Depends on concrete class
class OrderService {
  private db = new PostgresDatabase(); // tightly coupled

  async getOrder(id: string) {
    return this.db.query(`SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = '${id}'`);
  }
}
// To test OrderService, you need a real PostgreSQL database.
// To switch to MySQL, you must rewrite this class.
```

## The Solution: Depend on an Interface

```typescript
// Define an abstraction
interface Database {
  query<T>(sql: string, params?: unknown[]): Promise<T[]>;
}

// OrderService depends only on the interface
class OrderService {
  constructor(private db: Database) {}

  async getOrder(id: string) {
    return this.db.query<Order>('SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = $1', [id]);
  }
}

// Production: inject real DB
const service = new OrderService(new PostgresDatabase());

// Tests: inject mock
const mockDb: Database = {
  query: async () => [{ id: '1', total: 100 }],
};
const testService = new OrderService(mockDb);
```

## Benefits

```
✅ Testability — swap in a mock without touching OrderService
✅ Flexibility — switch PostgreSQL → SQLite → DynamoDB by swapping the class
✅ Parallel development — team A implements OrderService, team B implements Database
✅ Follows Dependency Inversion (the D in SOLID)
```

## In React / Next.js

```typescript
// Interface for any analytics provider
interface Analytics {
  track(event: string, props?: Record<string, unknown>): void;
}

// Component depends on the interface, not Segment/Amplitude directly
function SignupButton({ analytics }: { analytics: Analytics }) {
  return <button onClick={() => analytics.track('signup_clicked')}>Sign up</button>;
}
```

