## DTOs vs ViewModels in DDD

## The Mapping Chain

```
[Database / Domain Layer]
      ↓
  Domain Entity  (rich, has behaviour, enforces invariants)
      ↓  Application Service maps it
    DTO            (flat, serialisable, no behaviour)
      ↓  API response / frontend maps it
  ViewModel        (shaped for a specific UI view)
```

## Domain Entity

```typescript
// Rich — has behaviour and invariants
class Order {
  private status: OrderStatus;
  confirm(): void { /* business logic */ }
  get totalPrice(): Money { /* computation */ }
}
```

## DTO — crosses service boundary

```typescript
// Flat, safe to serialise across the wire
interface OrderDTO {
  id: string;
  userId: string;
  status: string;
  totalPrice: number;
  currency: string;
  createdAt: string; // ISO string — not a Date object
}

function toOrderDTO(order: Order): OrderDTO {
  return {
    id: order.id,
    userId: order.userId,
    status: order.status,
    totalPrice: order.totalPrice.amount,
    currency: order.totalPrice.currency,
    createdAt: order.createdAt.toISOString(),
  };
}
```

## ViewModel — shaped for the UI

```typescript
// Combines order + user data for an order detail page
interface OrderDetailViewModel {
  orderId: string;
  formattedTotal: string;    // '£49.99' — pre-formatted
  statusLabel: string;       // 'Confirmed' — human-readable
  customerName: string;      // denormalised from User
  itemCount: number;
}
```

## Rule of Thumb

```
DTO    → defined by the API contract (what the backend exposes)
ViewModel → defined by the UI requirements (what the page needs)
Never expose domain entities directly over an API
```

