## DRY and DIE Principles

## DRY — Don't Repeat Yourself

> Every piece of knowledge must have a single, unambiguous, authoritative representation within a system.
> — Andy Hunt & Dave Thomas, The Pragmatic Programmer

```typescript
// ❌ DRY violation — discount logic duplicated in two places
function applyWebDiscount(price: number, isPremium: boolean): number {
  return isPremium ? price * 0.8 : price;
}

function applyMobileDiscount(price: number, isPremium: boolean): number {
  return isPremium ? price * 0.8 : price; // same logic, different name
}

// ✅ Single representation
function applyPremiumDiscount(price: number, isPremium: boolean): number {
  return isPremium ? price * 0.8 : price;
}
```

## Where DRY Goes Wrong

```typescript
// Two functions that look identical but represent DIFFERENT concepts:
function validateUserEmail(email: string): boolean {
  return /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email);
}

function validateNewsletterEmail(email: string): boolean {
  return /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email);
}

// Tempting to merge — but if newsletter validation adds extra rules later
// (e.g. block disposable addresses), they need to diverge.
// Same code ≠ same knowledge.
```

## DIE — Duplication Is Evil

A stronger heuristic: treat any duplication as a code smell to investigate. Even if you don't abstract immediately, flag it.

## The Rule of Three

```
1 occurrence → write it inline
2 occurrences → consider abstracting, but check if concepts are truly the same
3 occurrences → extract; the pattern is clearly real
```

> "Prefer duplication over the wrong abstraction." — Sandi Metz

