## NULL vs Empty Values

## The Core Distinction

```
null / undefined  → "this thing does not exist"
empty []          → "this thing exists, but has no items"
```

## For Collections: Prefer Empty

```typescript
// ❌ Returns null for 'no results'
async function getUserOrders(userId: string): Promise<Order[] | null> {
  const orders = await db.query(...);
  return orders.length > 0 ? orders : null;
}

// Every caller must guard:
const orders = await getUserOrders(userId);
if (orders !== null) { // tedious
  orders.forEach(...);
}

// ✅ Empty array is always safe to iterate
async function getUserOrders(userId: string): Promise<Order[]> {
  const orders = await db.query(...);
  return orders; // [] if none
}

// Caller:
const orders = await getUserOrders(userId);
orders.forEach(...); // no null check needed
```

## For Single Objects: null/undefined Is Appropriate

```typescript
// 'Not found' is a meaningful state — returning null makes sense
async function findUserById(id: string): Promise<User | null> {
  const { data } = await supabase.from('users').select().eq('id', id).single();
  return data ?? null;
}

// Caller must handle both cases — which is correct:
const user = await findUserById(id);
if (!user) throw new NotFoundError();
```

## TypeScript: Make the Contract Explicit

```typescript
// Return type forces the caller to handle null
function getFeatureFlag(key: string): boolean | null { ... }

// Or use a discriminated union for richer semantics
type Result<T> = { found: true; value: T } | { found: false };
```

## Summary

```
List results    → return [] not null
Single lookup   → return T | null
Optional config → return T | undefined
Never           → throw on 'not found' unless it's truly exceptional
```

