## Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

## The Problem: Fat Interface

```typescript
// ❌ One interface forces all implementors to define methods they don't use
interface UserDataStore {
  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
  findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null>;
  save(user: User): Promise<void>;
  delete(id: string): Promise<void>;
  bulkImport(users: User[]): Promise<void>;  // only needed by admin import
  exportToCsv(): Promise<string>;             // only needed by reporting
}

// AdminImportService only needs bulkImport, but must implement everything else
class InMemoryUserStore implements UserDataStore {
  bulkImport(users: User[]) { /* only this is used */ }
  exportToCsv() { throw new Error('Not implemented'); } // ← ISP violation
  // ...all other stubs...
}
```

## Fixed: Segregated Interfaces

```typescript
// Each interface expresses a single capability
interface UserReader {
  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
  findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null>;
}

interface UserWriter {
  save(user: User): Promise<void>;
  delete(id: string): Promise<void>;
}

interface UserBulkImporter {
  bulkImport(users: User[]): Promise<void>;
}

interface UserExporter {
  exportToCsv(): Promise<string>;
}

// Services depend only on what they need:
class GetUserUseCase {
  constructor(private users: UserReader) {} // only needs reads
}

class AdminImportService {
  constructor(private importer: UserBulkImporter) {} // only needs bulk import
}
```

## Benefits

```
✅ Implementors only provide what they support
✅ Tests: mocks implement only the methods the test uses
✅ Stability: adding a method to UserExporter doesn't break GetUserUseCase
✅ Readability: the interface name + methods document the capability clearly
```

