## WebSockets vs REST API for Real-Time Data

## Decision Matrix

```
┌──────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬────────────┬────────────┐
│ Use Case                     │ REST/HTTP    │ SSE        │ WebSocket  │
├──────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────┼────────────┤
│ CRUD API                     │ ✅ Best      │ ❌         │ ❌         │
│ Occasional updates           │ ✅ Polling   │ ✅         │ Overkill   │
│ Server pushes only           │ ❌ Polling   │ ✅ Best    │ ✅         │
│ Live chat (bidirectional)    │ ❌           │ ❌         │ ✅ Best    │
│ Live collaboration (Figma)   │ ❌           │ ❌         │ ✅ Best    │
│ Stock ticker (server → client)│ ❌ Polling  │ ✅ Best    │ ✅         │
│ Multiplayer game             │ ❌           │ ❌         │ ✅ Best    │
│ File upload / download       │ ✅ Best      │ ❌         │ ❌         │
└──────────────────────────────┴──────────────┴────────────┴────────────┘
```

## REST Polling — When Good Enough

```typescript
// Acceptable if update frequency is low (every 30s+)
setInterval(async () => {
  const { data } = await fetch('/api/notifications');
  setNotifications(data);
}, 30_000);
```

## Server-Sent Events (SSE) — Server Push, Simple

```typescript
// Server
export async function GET() {
  const stream = new ReadableStream({ /* ... */ });
  return new Response(stream, {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream' },
  });
}

// Client
const sse = new EventSource('/api/stream');
sse.onmessage = (e) => setData(JSON.parse(e.data));

// Advantages: HTTP/1.1, auto-reconnect, firewall-friendly
// Limitation: server → client only
```

## WebSocket — Bidirectional

```typescript
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://api.example.com/chat');
ws.onmessage = (e) => addMessage(JSON.parse(e.data));
ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'message', text: 'hello' }));
// Client AND server can send at any time
```

## Summary Recommendation

```
Primary: use REST for everything you can
Server push (updates): SSE is simpler, prefer it over WebSocket
Bidirectional real-time: WebSocket
Fallback: long-polling if WebSocket is blocked (corporate proxies)
```
